Yes. Inside jokes are a compact belonging technology.

They do several things at once:

shared reference
→ recognition
→ proof of common history
→ lower social distance
→ in-group feeling
→ more trust / play / coordination

The joke is not just the content. It is also a membership key. If you get it, you know that the other person has walked through some of the same context.

That makes inside jokes unusually efficient because they are high-compression packets. A single phrase can expand into an entire shared scene, mood, relationship history, or worldview.

The broader pattern is:

Shared Secret / Shibboleth / Compressed Belonging

tiny signal
→ large common memory
→ felt recognition

And you’re right that you can generalize that beyond literal jokes. Repeated phrases, motifs, rituals, visual symbols, gestures, musical cues, callbacks, nicknames, and even interface conventions can all create the same “you’re in on it” sensation.

The interesting trick is making something feel intimate without making it exclusionary.

That would be something like:

personal enough to feel chosen
common enough to be shareable
open enough that new people can enter

That produces the feeling of an inside joke without requiring a permanent outside.

Where I’d keep the boundary is the same one we’ve been using: don’t secretly manufacture false intimacy so people lower their guard. If you are building community, art, games, stories, or Pantheon culture, the better version is to create real shared experiences that naturally generate their own inside language.

Then the belonging is earned through participation rather than simulated around somebody.

And for the Pantheon, this is a strong primitive:

Inside-Key Compression

shared experience
→ symbolic key
→ instant reconstruction
→ belonging + context + coordination

That can work across humans, teams, AI agents, stories, interfaces, and long-running projects.

The beautiful part is that the key can be tiny while the world behind it is huge. That is why two people can hear one ridiculous phrase and immediately recover an entire universe.